#N canvas 20 20 1400 780 12;
#X text 20 20 t_ctlout -- a stand-in for [ctlout] that prints every control change the patch emits \, with the controller and the channel. tools/phase9-assert.sh rewrites [ctlout] to [t_ctlout] in a SCRATCH COPY \; nothing in Cut It/ is touched and this file is never deployed., f 84;
#X text 20 140 ⚠️ THIS IS THE ONE THAT CARRIES CREATION ARGS IN THE REAL PATCH. u_tempo has [ctlout 123 33] -- All Notes Off on the 404's bank A -- so a rewriter whose regex demands the class name at END OF LINE silently skips it \, which is exactly what phase6-assert.sh does. Phase 9's takes a trailing argument list \, and this stub reads both args so the rewritten box keeps its meaning., f 84;
#X text 20 300 creation arg 1: the controller. creation arg 2: the channel. inlet 0: value \, HOT. inlet 1: controller \, cold. inlet 2: channel \, cold. outlets: none. Prints CTLOUT: <value> <controller> <channel> -- INLET ORDER \, not the order a human would say it. A capture is parsed positionally \, so the order only has to be stated once and kept., f 84;
#X obj 20 500 inlet;
#X obj 300 500 inlet;
#X obj 580 500 inlet;
#X obj 20 570 pack f f f;
#X obj 20 640 print CTLOUT;
#X obj 900 420 loadbang;
#X obj 900 470 t b b;
#X obj 900 530 f \$1;
#X obj 1100 530 f \$2;
#X obj 1100 580 max 1;
#X text 900 640 THE TRIGGER IS NOT DECORATION. Both defaults land in COLD inlets of the same pack \, and a bare fan-out from loadbang would leave the order to creation order -- invisible in the file. The channel default is max 1 for the same reason as t_noteout: a missing arg answers 0 \, and the real object treats a bare [ctlout] as channel 1., f 60;
#X connect 3 0 6 0;
#X connect 4 0 6 1;
#X connect 5 0 6 2;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 8 0 9 0;
#X connect 9 1 10 0;
#X connect 9 0 11 0;
#X connect 10 0 6 1;
#X connect 11 0 12 0;
#X connect 12 0 6 2;
